# Minimal reproduction for schedule.rs's piped-input branch propagating raw channel
# disconnection as a hard error instead of treating it as ordinary stream exhaustion.
#
# `schedule`'s piped branch does `output.send(input.read()?)` in a loop with no `--once`
# and no bound on the input; once the input stream (`seq 0 2`, 3 rows) is exhausted,
# `input.read()` returns Err (a crossbeam RecvError, since the row channel carries plain
# `Row`, not `Result<Row, _>`, and can't express "there was an upstream error" -- it can
# only ever mean "no more rows"). Every other consumer of `TableStreamReader::read()` in
# the codebase treats this as ordinary end-of-stream (`while let Ok(row) = ... { }`);
# schedule.rs is the one place that instead propagates it with `?`, turning normal
# exhaustion into a spurious "receiving on an empty and disconnected channel" error.
#
# Unlike the Command::deserialize bug (tests/command_value_via_pup.crush), this can't be
# made observable via a plain stdout diff, nor caught with try/catch: `schedule` is a
# non-last pipeline stage here (piped to `echo`), and Job::eval only tracks/joins the
# *last* stage's thread -- a non-last stage's spawned thread, and any error it produces,
# is never joined at all, so it never becomes a catchable CrushResult::Err and
# source() never aborts. There's no synchronous point to hang a "did it succeed" marker
# on, and no builtin exposes a script's own stderr as inspectable data either. See
# test-gaps.md's `Job::eval()` thread-join-gap entry.
seq 0 2 | schedule $(duration:of milliseconds=1) | echo
